A wedding planning timeline is a calendar run in reverse: you start from the big day and work back to the first decision, the date. Ours has one particular feature: it is written from a wedding venue. At Château de la Huberdière, near Amboise, we see every year the order in which dates actually go, and it is not always the one the magazines suggest.

Here is the wedding checklist month by month, with what to book first, the formalities that do not forgive delay, and a typical timeline for the day itself.

## When should you start your wedding planning timeline?

For a Saturday between May and September, start twelve to eighteen months ahead: that is the window in which wedding venues and sought-after photographers fill up.

This lead time is not a universal rule. It depends on the season and the day. A June Saturday at a Loire château is often booked more than a year in advance; an October Friday or a February weekend can still be found six months out. If your date is flexible, you gain months of preparation, and often part of the budget.

Our advice: before filling in any spreadsheet, settle two figures together, the overall budget and the number of guests to within ten. Every later decision flows from them, starting with the venue. A timeline without those two figures is just a wish list.

## 18 to 12 months before: what should you book first?

Book the venue first, before the caterer and the dress, because on its own it sets the date, the capacity and a good part of the budget.

Visit two or three venues, not ten. Go at the planned time of the ceremony if you can: light, shade and noise change everything. Ask what is included in the price, what is not, and what happens if it rains.

![The château's stone terrace overlooking a pond, a balustraded staircase and wooded grounds behind under a summer sky](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-01.webp "The terrace looks down over the pond and the grounds: this is where the drinks reception takes place. On your visit, come at the planned ceremony time to see where the sun falls.")

At la Huberdière, the estate is booked for exclusive use by the weekend, from Friday to Sunday, at 4,000 € to 8,000 € depending on the season, with the 21 beds included. Our article on [château wedding cost](/en/blog/chateau-wedding-cost) breaks down the other items one by one.

In the same period:

- set the date and the overall budget;
- draw up a first guest list;
- visit and book the wedding venue;
- book the photographer, much in demand on summer Saturdays.

## 12 to 9 months before: which suppliers should you book?

Once the venue is signed, book the caterer, then the music and the celebrant if you are planning a humanist ceremony, as these are the first to run out.

The caterer is the largest item in a wedding budget, at 80 to 180 € per guest. Check their minimum number of covers before you sign: some caterers impose one, often around fifty. At the château you keep your choice of caterer, and we recommend the ones we have already worked with.

Think about the wine as well. If you bring your own caterer, they set the corkage on any wine you supply: have it written into the quote before you sign. When we provide the meal ourselves, corkage is 8 € per bottle. Choosing your wines is still one more line on the checklist, and the winemakers of Vouvray and Montlouis are better visited eight months ahead than eight days ahead.

To tick off in this period:

- caterer, DJ or musicians, celebrant;
- send the save the dates;
- choose your witnesses;
- start looking at the dress and the suit, which need several months of making and alterations.

## 9 to 6 months before: how do you house your guests?

Sort out guest accommodation between nine and six months before the wedding, before the hotels and holiday cottages in the area are taken by the season's weekends.

This is the line couples most often forget, yet it decides the mood of the morning after. At the château, the 10 rooms and their 21 beds go to close family and witnesses: they wake up where the party ended. For everyone else, we provide a list of partner holiday cottages and hotels less than ten minutes away. Send it with the save the date, not with the invitation.

In the same period, decide on the form of the ceremony. If you want it humanist, in the grounds or on the terrace, our guide to the [humanist wedding ceremony at a château](/en/blog/humanist-wedding-ceremony-chateau) covers the order of service, choosing a celebrant and the rain plan. Order the rings and plan the honeymoon too.

## 6 to 3 months before: what are the town hall formalities?

If you are marrying legally in France, file your wedding dossier at the town hall three to four months ahead, because the banns must be published for at least ten days and some documents expire.

The French government site service-public.fr sets out the rules. You marry in a commune where one of you, or one of your parents, has their home or residence. Full copies of birth certificates must be less than three months old when you file them if they are issued in France: do not request them too early. If you sign a marriage contract, the notary gives you a certificate to hand to the town hall, and that appointment comes first.

The rest of the period:

- send the invitations, four to six months ahead;
- menu tasting with the caterer;
- hair and make-up trials;
- order the furniture: at the château, round tables and reception chairs are available for 15 € per person, which saves an outside rental.

## 3 to 1 month before: how do you build the wedding day timeline?

Write the wedding day timeline three months ahead, hour by hour, then send it to every supplier so that everyone works from the same version.

Here is the typical timeline we see working for a summer wedding:

1. **11 am**: town hall, then a light lunch with close family;
2. **3.30 pm**: humanist ceremony in the grounds, 45 minutes;
3. **5 pm**: drinks reception on the terrace, until 7.30 pm;
4. **8 pm**: dinner in the great vaulted hall;
5. **11 pm**: the cake, then the first dance.

![A reception table laid in the great vaulted hall, crystal glasses, folded napkins, a white bouquet and gilded chairs](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-09.webp "The seating plan is finalised a month ahead, once replies are in. A round table seats eight to ten guests: count the tables before you count the flowers.")

Add thirty minutes of slack between each key moment. Group photos overrun, speeches too, and dinner always pays the price. The rest of the period: chase guests who have not replied, the seating plan, final fittings, and written confirmation of times with every supplier.

## The last week and the day before: what should you check?

The last week is for confirming, not deciding: any choice still open a week out will be made in a rush, and therefore badly.

Pay off the suppliers who ask for it, prepare envelopes for those paid on the day, and hand a witness an emergency kit: thread, needle, plasters, safety pins, painkillers.

![The bride and groom kiss in front of the château's red door, framed with ivy, on the gravel of the courtyard](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-06.webp "The red front door makes one of the most requested couple photos. Build it into the timeline, before the ceremony or at sunset.")

This is where an exclusive-use weekend makes the difference. You arrive on the Friday, the florist and the caterer set up without rushing, the first guests have dinner with you, and the wet-weather decision is made calmly the evening before. On Saturday, all that is left is to live the day.

## What does an exclusive-use château take off your checklist?

An exclusive-use château takes three lines off the checklist: housing close family, the wet-weather fallback and sharing the venue with another event.

The 21 beds house the family on site. The great vaulted hall and the Orangery take over from the grounds at no extra cost and with no marquee to hire. And the 14-hectare estate is yours alone all weekend: no other wedding shares the car park or the dance floor.

It adds one, honestly: the logistics of an entire venue. If you would rather hand them over, our optional 2,500 € coordination package takes on the wedding planner's role: five follow-up video calls, liaising with suppliers and coordinating the day itself, by the people who live on the estate. For guests who want to stay on, our guide to [sleeping in a Loire Valley château](/en/blog/sleep-in-a-loire-chateau) offers ideas, and our [boutique hotel rooms near Amboise](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise) stay open after the celebration.

Have a date in mind? Write to us from our [château wedding](/en/chateau-wedding-loire) page: we will send you a proposal within 24 hours.
